          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus treats “bow” contextually: the rainbow signifies God’s covenant and mercy, while the weapon signifies warfare, military power, conquest, or judgment; breaking the weapon signifies defeated might or the end of war.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book unifies these senses, identifying the bow as a kingdom’s covenant together with the power constituted or joined by that covenant; breaking it dissolves the kingdom’s organized might.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Bow = covenant sign when a rainbow, or military power and warfare when a weapon.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Bow = a kingdom’s covenantal union and its associated power.\",\"core_relation\":\"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus generally keeps covenant-rainbow and military-weapon symbolism as context-dependent senses. The book combines them into one kingdom-centered identification and reclassifies ordinary weapon-bow passages as expressions of covenantally joined political power, not merely warfare or might.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction in identifying the bow with covenant or power, both of which the consensus recognizes. The material difference is the book’s claim that these belong to a single kingdom-covenant symbolism across the cited cases.\",\"rationale\":\"The entry is not novel because covenant, military power, and the breaking of power are all established consensus meanings. It is not a match because the consensus does not ordinarily identify every bow with a kingdom’s covenant or treat covenant and military might as one symbolic complex. The book therefore preserves recognizable consensus categories while materially reclassifying and unifying their cases.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts separately support the rainbow as a covenant token and the weapon-bow as might that can be broken, but they do not explicitly establish that a weapon-bow represents a kingdom’s covenant or that covenant is what joins its power. The kingdom-centered synthesis is inferred rather than directly demonstrated by the listed verses.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
